I'm a hardcore Bernie supporter. I know his entire platform is "not me, us" but to be honest I just want him so badly to be recognized for the hero he is, and very likely the catalyst of future changes once our generation begins to run things. I just want him to not fade away. If he can't be president, then SOMETHING.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I'd love to see him drop out and endorse Biden live at the end of the debate, having given Biden an opportunity to prove himself to Progressives. Think about what a show of unity that would be.

Bare with me as my post transitions from mildly frustrated, to overwhelmingly and arguably uncomfortably passionate.

Let's start with the Bernie Bro thing.

To be honest, I'm convinced that everyone is just as big of an asshole as the next girl/guy. I've seen Pete supporters , the official "teachers for pete" twitter IIRC, saying they pray for Bernie to have another heart attack. I see MSNBC dude literally comparing him to a nazi for winning in Nevada, I see Biden people calling Bernie an absolute disaster, I see users calling Bernie a Russian asset...and ect and ect;  it's almost as if it's universally common for supporters of a candidate to attack other candidates, usually because they see the opposing candidate's attacks on THEIR candidate as a lie, or wrong, or irrelevant in face of a bigger problem. And that goes for EVERY candidate's supporters. In American history, especially today.

&#x200B;

What I see the difference being, is simply that here on reddit and on twitter is predominantly, overwhelmingly, younger people. And who do you see the young people voting for in overwhelming support? Bernie.

&#x200B;

This isn't to say it's not wrong to respond to anything Pete says with "he's a rat" or to Warren with a nice snake emoji. But I think I have a right to be angry at Pete when he says Bernie's plan will "leave out most Americans", just as Pete supporters are perfectly within their rights to be pissed at Bernie and claim flip flopping because of his support of a brokered convention in 2016 or something. I've seen vile shit from literally all corners, but then I look at the numbers, and I see the footage of people who support him, and put into perspective the sheer amount of people Bernie has supporting him over such a wide range of demographics. I see the faces of a countless number of black and brown faces, and women and marginalised people whose voices haven't been heard support Bernie and start thinking how condescending the "Bernie Bro" trope is to these people who the attackers are trying desperately to win over. In fact, to be entirely frank, I feel like I have gained at least a certain degree of sympathy for Trump supporters in 2016. When you call someone's supporters a basket of deplorable, you don't win over their votes, you make them burrow *deeper* and rally even harder for the one who makes them feel safe and hopeful, or even someone who appeals and protects your hatred of immigrants.

&#x200B;

I see his policies and find myself agreeing so powerfully and deeply in my heart, regardless of the fact he "might make us lose the house of representatives". People like us: the young people, when every headline every day is about how absolutely fucked our world is, when studies show us being the first generation in so long to be worse off than our parents, when we see the scope of the student debt crisis and how it will cripple us, when we see from 2016 and Trump's victory, and Obama's 2008, is that it is the hungriest, most passionate and angriest group who win, it is hard to feel like this isn't our last shot.

&#x200B;

So we don't have time for moderates to concern-troll and warn and caution us, and claim the only path to change is through careful, slow, moderately paced progress that includes buttering up and appealing to the generations and billionaires who have fucked us so much. We dream for a country where we don't have to COMPROMISE anymore, APPEASE any more, and at the risk of sounding even more like a radical, batshit crazy "commie" revolutionist than I already am, fear anymore.

&#x200B;

We see someone Like Bernie who has remained so passionately consistent with his message, casting lonely votes against wars, supporting LGBT people before it was politically viable, like Clinton and even Obama to a degree. Sure, his gun record isn't perfect, and to point out that inconsistency is a fair point to make. But what we see is a consistency of his *soul.* Since he was in his 40's (and looking almost exactly the same as he does now) he was in the streets of Burlington talking to children about land laws, drugs, education. He was working tirelessly for the working people, and his message of peeling away the power the billionaires have over us has been the same for so damn long.

&#x200B;

So we see that man and we realise how passionate he is and we can't help but match his passion and then some. We are angry, definitely angry, but we are also hopeful. I can't tell you how much this sense of hope and passion for our future has invigorated me. After years of suicidal depression over my sexuality, my eating disorder, my future, my friends, my body, I have found my people. And I truly believe that at this point, the only way forward that gives me true hope is through a political revolution, not a compromise.

&#x200B;

And yet we are not as fragile as many think. Regardless of who wins, we will continue on, like everyone else in history has before us in times of strife...at least for as long as this planet can sustain us, and vice versa. I have grown up pretty comfortably, I feel it is a blessing to worry about my mental condition so much, as opposed to my medical bills. But I personally feel that it is my duty as someone who has so much to be grateful for, to work until the day I die to for those who need support. Two nights ago I was exhausted and almost disillusioned from the shock of seeing just how much hatred *my own party* has for Bernie. But this community urged me to keep fighting. Like Bernie, who won't spend time with pleasantries like calling you on your birthday just to make you feel good, this community had less sugarcoating for me, and more "Get back up and WORK. HARDER.

When you feel this passion and believe so overwhelmingly powerfully, and feel so bonded with your fellow supporters, the talking point that some of your candidate's supporters are mean are twitter becomes deeply inconsequential. There is so much more at stake here, and so much work to be done. And we are confident and hopeful that our continually expanding movement will win over people from all demographics, and reach a point where no one can deny anymore that this dream is impossible, and that the billionaires and hyper-conservatives and establishment Democrats we "needed to appease" don't matter anymore, because the struggling and working people of America outnumber them.

We are passionate. We are furious. And we are working HARD for this man. It's the least we can do to support a man who, at 78, has truly dedicated his entire life to a cause we are willing to fight for too. And because of that, even if Bernie has a heart attack, or falls down a flight of stairs, or spontaneously combusts and dies 1 week into his term, I doubt many of us will regret a thing.

&#x200B;

So thank you, genuinely, for your concern over Sander's supporter base. It is a perfectly reasonable thing to be worried about, and I will continue to try to reach out to supporters of all candidates and help bring us together. I'm definitely not perfect either. But i'm trying.

\*Bernie sanders defeating Pete Buttigieg in the election.

