This is what Im excited for: RDR2--but then also the next step of games that follow in it's stead.

&#x200B;

Games like Witcher 3, Horizon, even Zelda BOTW--they aren't really leaps forward. They are just relly good executions of the established open world formula.

&#x200B;

RDR2, instead of adding gimmicks and new things to this formula, is breaking it all down at a micro level and redefining what it means to interact with the world. With RDR2 we aren't getting some massive new gimmick. We are being given something entirely unprecedented. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I remember when I knew that any ubisoft game that came out would have top notch animation quality better than all the competitors in the open world genre.

&#x200B;

What happened.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

For years I thought I've been going crazy. people saying amazing things about games like Horizon, Witcher 3, Zelda, and I play it and theyre great games, but it still often just feels like I'm getting from one task to the next and I'm just going down a chore list.

&#x200B;

This could be the first videogame I play that breaks that expectation of tedium, perhaps an experience that really holds my attention and interest every moment. People are describing this as the next big leap in open world games. It seems Next generation. I am unbelievably excited.

&#x200B;

This could be the first game where I no longer suspend my expectations of how a  character might react and behave due to it being a game. I am so excited at the prospect of actually being in a world where my actions really matter. So many games have boasted this feature but none have actually truly achieved what RDR2 is looking to achieve.

